FILES = {'docs/adr/0011-modules-share-a-namespace.md': '# ADR 0011: Imported files are merged into one namespace\nStatus: accepted for v0.2\n\n`import` loads the file, and its functions, constants, classes and enums join the program.\nName clashes are errors. This is small, simple, and makes every message point at the right file.\n`pub` is parsed but not enforced yet. Qualified names (`util.add`) and real visibility are planned.\n', 'docs/adr/0012-errors-without-unwinding.md': '# ADR 0012: Errors use a pending-error slot, not stack unwinding\nStatus: accepted\n\n`throw` stores the error and jumps; callers check a flag after calls that can fail.\nIt needs no unwinding tables, keeps the runtime tiny, costs nothing for code that cannot fail,\nand lets reference counting release everything on the error path.\n', 'docs/adr/0013-optionals-for-heap-types.md': '# ADR 0013: Optionals only for heap types in v0.2\nStatus: accepted (limitation)\n\n`none` is a null reference, so `string?`, `[T]?`, `{string: T}?` and `Class?` cost nothing.\n`int?` would need boxing; it is planned. Narrowing after `x != none` is planned; until then use `!` and `??`.\n', 'docs/adr/0014-maps-have-string-keys.md': '# ADR 0014: Maps have string keys, in insertion order\nStatus: accepted (limitation)\n\nString keys cover JSON-like data and configuration, and keep the runtime small. Insertion order\nmakes output predictable (good for tests and AI agents). Integer keys are planned.\n', 'docs/adr/0015-named-arguments.md': '# ADR 0015: Named arguments for every call\nStatus: accepted\n\nConstructors take fields by name (`Account(owner: "Asha")`), and functions and methods accept\nnamed arguments too. Named calls are self-documenting for people and for AI agents.\nPositional arguments come first.\n', 'docs/adr/0016-fmt-only-changes-whitespace.md': '# ADR 0016: `kode fmt` only changes whitespace\nStatus: accepted\n\nThe formatter re-indents lines and collapses blank lines. It never rewrites tokens, so it cannot\nchange meaning and cannot lose a comment. A fuller formatter that also normalizes spacing inside\nlines can come later, once comments are part of the syntax tree.\n', 'docs/agents/cheatsheet.md': '# Kode cheat sheet (v0.2)\n\nCompiled language, one statement per line, no semicolons. Types: `int float bool string`,\n`[T]` list, `{string: T}` map, `T?` optional (string, list, map, class), classes, enums.\n\n```\n// comment            /* block */\nx = 5                 // declares; type is fixed; block-scoped\ny: float = 2.5        // explicit type\nconst MAX = 100       // plain literal only\nx += 1                // also -= *= /= %=\ns = "Hi ${name}, ${x + 1}"        // interpolation; escapes \\n \\t \\" \\\\ \\$\na = [1, 2, 3]         a[0]   a.add(4)   a.len()   for x in a   for i, x in a\nm = {"k": 1}          m["k"] m["z"] = 2   "k" in m   m.has("k")   m.keys()   for k, v in m\n```\n\nOperators: `+ - * / %`  `== != < <= > >=`  `and or not`  `??`  `?.`  `!`  `in`.\n`int / int` truncates. Numbers never mix: use `int(x)`, `float(x)`. Conditions must be `bool`.\n\n```\nif a > b { ... } else if a == b { ... } else { ... }\nwhile cond { ... }          for i in 0..10 { ... }     for i in 0..=10 { ... }\nbreak   continue            match v { 1 => print("one")  _ => print("other") }\n\nfn add(a: int, b: int) -> int { return a + b }      // return type required when it returns a value\nfn sq(x: int) -> int => x * x                        // one-expression body\nfn greet(name: string) { print("hi", name) }         // returns nothing\ngreet(name: "Asha")                                  // named arguments\n\nclass Account {\n    owner: string\n    balance: int = 0\n    fn deposit(n: int) { balance += n }              // fields by plain name\n}\nacct = Account(owner: "Asha")   acct.deposit(5)   acct.balance\n\nenum Color { Red, Green }       c = Color.Red        // compare with ==, use with match\n\nu: Account? = none     u?.owner ?? "nobody"    u!.owner\nthrow "message"        throw Error(message: "x", code: 7)\nv = f() catch e { print(e.message)  -1 }            // last line is the value\ntry { f() } catch e { print(e.message) }\n\nimport "./util"        from "./util" import helper   // modules share one namespace\ntest "name" { assert(1 + 1 == 2) }                   // run with: kode test\n```\n\nBuilt-ins: `print(...)`, `len(x)`, `int(x)`, `float(x)`, `string(x)`, `assert(cond)`.\nCommands: `kode run|build|check|test|fmt|explain|mir`. Use `kode check --format=json` for errors\nand `kode explain E0201` for help. Strict rules: no implicit conversions, no truthy values, no\ninheritance, no semicolons, one statement per line.\n', 'docs/design/errors.md': "# How errors work\n\n`throw` does not unwind the stack. It stores the error object in one global slot of the runtime\nand jumps to the nearest handler in the same function, or out of the function.\n\n1. The checker works out which functions can fail: a function throws directly (`throw`,\n   `assert`) or calls one that does. Calls to functions that cannot fail cost nothing extra\n   (recursive Fibonacci runs at full speed).\n2. After a call to a function that can fail, the compiled code calls `kode_err_pending`.\n   If an error is set it jumps to the `catch` block of the innermost `try` or `catch`, or to\n   the function's error exit.\n3. The error exit releases the function's variables and returns a default value, so the caller\n   sees the pending error too.\n4. `catch` takes the error out of the slot (`kode_err_take`), so it belongs to the catch variable.\n5. At the top of the program, an error that is still pending prints `kode: error: message`\n   and exits with code 1.\n\nErrors are ordinary `Error` objects (a message and a code), so they are reference counted\nand freed like everything else.\n", 'docs/spec/08-collections.md': '# 8. Lists and maps\n\n```\nxs = [3, 1, 2]                 # [int]\nxs.add(10)                     # append\nprint(xs[0], xs.len(), len(xs))\nxs[1] = 99                     # change an item\n\nnames: [string] = []           # an empty list needs a declared type\n\nages = {"asha": 30, "ravi": 25}    # {string: int}, keys are always strings\nages["maya"] = 28\nages["asha"] += 1\nprint("ravi" in ages, ages.has("zed"), ages.len())\nfor k, v in ages { print(k, v) }   # insertion order\nfor k in ages { print(k) }\nks = ages.keys()                   # a new [string]\n\nfor x in xs { print(x) }           # items\nfor i, x in xs { print(i, x) }     # index and item\n```\n\n- Item types are `int`, `float`, `bool`, `string`, classes, and other lists and maps.\n  All items must have the same type (E0213).\n- Index out of range, and a missing map key, stop the program with an error that names the line.\n- Lists and maps are reference counted like strings; `b = a` shares the same list.\n- Not yet: slicing, removing items, sorting, `map`/`filter`, integer map keys, printing a whole list.\n', 'docs/spec/09-classes-enums.md': '# 9. Classes, enums and match\n\n```\nclass Account {\n    owner: string\n    balance: int = 0                 # default value\n    tags: [string] = []\n\n    fn deposit(amount: int) {\n        balance += amount            # fields are used by their plain name\n    }\n\n    fn summary() -> string => "${owner}: ${balance}"\n}\n\nacct = Account(owner: "Asha")        # named arguments\nb = Account("Maya", 7)               # positional also works, in field order\nacct.deposit(50)\nacct.balance = 10\nprint(acct.summary())\nother = acct                         # the same object, not a copy\n```\n\n- A class is a reference type: `other = acct` shares one object. Objects are freed when the last reference goes.\n- A field without a default must be given when the object is created (E0216).\n- Inside a method a field is used by its plain name, and sibling methods are called by plain name.\n  A parameter may not have the same name as a field (E0228).\n- Named arguments work for functions, methods and constructors. Positional ones come first (E0215).\n- No inheritance (ADR 0006). Use composition: a class holds other classes.\n- Equality `==` on class values compares identity.\n- Built-in class `Error` has `message: string` and `code: int = 0`.\n\n## Enums and match\n\n```\nenum Color { Red, Green, Blue }\n\nc = Color.Green\nmatch c {\n    Color.Red => print("red")\n    Color.Green => {\n        print("green")\n    }\n    _ => print("other")\n}\n```\n\n- `match` works on `int`, `string`, `bool` and enums. It must cover every case or end with `_ =>` (E0224).\n- Enum values are compared with `==`. Printing an enum is not supported yet.\n- `match` is a statement in v0.2, not an expression.\n', 'docs/spec/10-optionals-errors.md': '# 10. Optionals and errors\n\n## Optionals\n\n```\nclass User {\n    name: string\n    nickname: string? = none\n}\n\nu: User? = none\nprint(u == none)\nname = u?.name ?? "guest"       # ?. stops at none, ?? gives a default\nfirst = u!                       # stop with an error if it is none\n```\n\n- `T?` can hold a value of type `T` or `none`. It works for `string`, lists, maps and classes.\n  `int?`, `float?` and `bool?` are not supported yet (E0219).\n- A value that may be none cannot be used with `.` or passed where `T` is needed (E0220).\n  Use `?.`, `??` or `!`.\n- `?.` works on fields whose type is a heap type (string, list, map, class).\n- Not yet: narrowing after `if x != none`. Use `x!` or `??`.\n\n## Errors\n\n```\nfn parse(s: string) -> int {\n    if s == "bad" {\n        throw "not a number: ${s}"                # short for Error(message: ...)\n    }\n    if s == "neg" {\n        throw Error(message: "negative", code: 7)\n    }\n    return 42\n}\n\nv = parse("bad") catch e {          # handle one expression\n    print(e.message)\n    -1                              # the value of the catch block\n}\n\ntry {                               # handle a block\n    parse("bad")\n} catch err {\n    print(err.message, err.code)\n}\n```\n\n- An error that is not caught leaves the function and goes to its caller.\n  At the top of the program it prints `kode: error: message` and exits with code 1.\n- The block after `catch` must end with a value (when the call has one), or with `return`,\n  `break`, `continue` or `throw` (E0227).\n- `assert(cond)` throws an `Error("assertion failed at line N")`.\n- Index and key errors, division by zero and `!` on none stop the program; they are not catchable yet.\n- Not yet: `finally`, custom error classes.\n', 'docs/spec/11-modules-tests-tools.md': '# 11. Modules, tests and tools\n\n## Imports\n\n```\nimport "./util"                       # all items of util.kode\nfrom "./models/user" import User, make_user\n```\n\n- The path is relative to the importing file; `.kode` is added.\n- A module may contain only `fn`, `const`, `class`, `enum` (and imports).\n  Top-level code in a module is an error (E0403).\n- In v0.2 **all files share one namespace**. A name defined twice is an error (E0301).\n  `pub` is accepted for the future but not enforced yet. A `from ... import` list is checked\n  (E0405) but does not hide other names.\n- Cycles are an error (E0402). A file imported twice is loaded once.\n\n## Tests\n\n```\ntest "add works" {\n    assert(add(2, 3) == 5)\n}\n```\n\n`kode test file.kode` (or a folder) runs every `test` block and prints `PASS name` or\n`FAIL name: message`, then `N passed, M failed`. The exit code is 1 when a test fails.\nA failing `assert` stops only its own test. Top-level code is not run in test mode.\n\n## Tools\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `kode fmt <files or folders>` | fix indentation and blank lines; `--check` only reports |\n| `kode explain E0201` | longer explanation of an error code with an example |\n| `kode check --format=json` | structured errors for editors and AI agents |\n| `kode mir file.kode` | show the compiler\'s middle layer |\n\n`kode fmt` only changes whitespace at the start and end of lines and collapses blank lines. It never changes what a program means and keeps all comments.\n', 'examples/bank.kode': '// Classes, maps, lists, enums, match and errors in one program\nenum Kind { Deposit, Withdraw }\n\nclass Account {\n    owner: string\n    balance: int = 0\n    history: [string] = []\n\n    fn apply(kind: Kind, amount: int) {\n        match kind {\n            Kind.Deposit => {\n                balance += amount\n            }\n            Kind.Withdraw => {\n                if amount > balance {\n                    throw Error(message: "insufficient funds for ${owner}", code: 402)\n                }\n                balance -= amount\n            }\n        }\n        history.add("${amount}")\n    }\n}\n\naccounts = {"asha": Account(owner: "Asha"), "ravi": Account(owner: "Ravi", balance: 50)}\n\naccounts["asha"].apply(Kind.Deposit, 100)\naccounts["ravi"].apply(Kind.Withdraw, 20)\n\naccounts["ravi"].apply(Kind.Withdraw, 500) catch e {\n    print("failed:", e.message, e.code)\n}\n\nfor name, acct in accounts {\n    print(name, acct.balance, len(acct.history))\n}\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0110_for_var.err': 'E0110\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0110_for_var.kode': 'for 1 in 0..3 {\n}\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0112_assign_target.err': 'E0112\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0112_assign_target.kode': '1 = 2\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0114_class_member.err': 'E0114\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0114_class_member.kode': 'class A {\n    5\n}\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0115_map_key.err': 'E0115\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0115_map_key.kode': 'x: {int: int} = {}\nprint(len(x))\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0116_import_path.err': 'E0116\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0116_import_path.kode': 'import 5\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0203_print_list.err': 'E0203\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0203_print_list.kode': 'print([1])\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0211_not_indexable.err': 'E0211\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0211_not_indexable.kode': 'x = 5\nprint(x[0])\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0212_index_type.err': 'E0212\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0212_index_type.kode': 'xs = [1]\nprint(xs["a"])\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0213_mixed_list.err': 'E0213\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0213_mixed_list.kode': 'xs = [1, "a"]\nprint(len(xs))\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0214_cannot_loop.err': 'E0214\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0214_cannot_loop.kode': 'for i in 5 {\n}\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0215_named_arg.err': 'E0215\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0215_named_arg.kode': 'fn f(a: int) -> int => a\nprint(f(b: 1))\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0216_missing_arg.err': 'E0216\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0216_missing_arg.kode': 'class A {\n    x: int\n}\na = A()\nprint(a.x)\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0217_unknown_field.err': 'E0217\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0217_unknown_field.kode': 'class A {\n    x: int = 1\n}\na = A()\nprint(a.y)\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0218_no_fields.err': 'E0218\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0218_no_fields.kode': 'x = 5\nprint(x.y)\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0219_optional_int.err': 'E0219\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0219_optional_int.kode': 'x: int? = none\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0220_maybe_none.err': 'E0220\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0220_maybe_none.kode': 'class A {\n    x: int = 1\n}\nfn f(a: A?) -> int {\n    return a.x\n}\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0221_coalesce.err': 'E0221\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0221_coalesce.kode': 'x = 5\nprint(x ?? 3)\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0222_empty_list.err': 'E0222\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0222_empty_list.kode': 'xs = []\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0223_match_type.err': 'E0223\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0223_match_type.kode': 'x = 1.5\nmatch x {\n    _ => print(1)\n}\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0224_not_exhaustive.err': 'E0224\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0224_not_exhaustive.kode': 'x = 1\nmatch x {\n    1 => print(1)\n}\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0225_unknown_variant.err': 'E0225\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0225_unknown_variant.kode': 'enum C { A }\nx = C.B\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0226_throw_type.err': 'E0226\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0226_throw_type.kode': 'throw 5\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0227_catch_value.err': 'E0227\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0227_catch_value.kode': 'fn f() -> int => 1\nx = f() catch e {\n    print(e.message)\n}\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0228_param_field.err': 'E0228\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0228_param_field.kode': 'class A {\n    x: int\n    fn f(x: int) {\n    }\n}\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0401_import_missing.err': 'E0401\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0401_import_missing.kode': 'import "./nope"\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0402_import_cycle.err': 'E0402\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0402_import_cycle.kode': 'import "./lib/cycle_a"\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0403_module_code.err': 'E0403\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0403_module_code.kode': 'import "./lib/has_code"\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0405_missing_name.err': 'E0405\n', 'tests/compile-fail/e0405_missing_name.kode': 'from "./lib/ok" import nothing\n', 'tests/compile-fail/lib/cycle_a.kode': 'import "./cycle_b"\nfn a() -> int => 1\n', 'tests/compile-fail/lib/cycle_b.kode': 'import "./cycle_a"\nfn b() -> int => 2\n', 'tests/compile-fail/lib/has_code.kode': 'print("not allowed in a module")\n', 'tests/compile-fail/lib/ok.kode': 'fn something() -> int => 1\n', 'tests/kode-test/failing.kode': 'test "one" {\n    assert(1 == 1)\n}\n\ntest "two" {\n    assert(1 == 2)\n}\n\ntest "three" {\n    xs = [1, 2]\n    assert(len(xs) == 2)\n}\n', 'tests/kode-test/passing.kode': 'fn add(a: int, b: int) -> int => a + b\n\ntest "add works" {\n    assert(add(2, 3) == 5)\n}\n\ntest "strings" {\n    s = "a" + "b"\n    assert(s == "ab")\n    assert(len(s) == 2)\n}\n', 'tests/run-pass/classes.kode': 'class Account {\n    owner: string\n    balance: int = 0\n    tags: [string] = []\n\n    fn deposit(amount: int) {\n        balance += amount\n    }\n\n    fn summary() -> string {\n        return "${owner}: ${balance}"\n    }\n\n    fn rich() -> bool => balance > 100\n}\n\nclass Point {\n    x: float\n    y: float\n    fn len2() -> float => x * x + y * y\n}\n\nacct = Account(owner: "Asha")\nacct.deposit(50)\nacct.deposit(75)\nprint(acct.summary(), acct.rich(), acct.balance)\nacct.balance = 10\nacct.owner = "Ravi"\nprint(acct.summary())\nother = acct\nother.deposit(5)\nprint(acct.balance)\np = Point(x: 3.0, y: 4.0)\nprint(p.len2(), p.x)\nacct.tags.add("vip")\nprint(len(acct.tags), acct.tags[0])\nb = Account("Maya", 7)\nprint(b.summary())\nfn make(n: string) -> Account => Account(owner: n, balance: 1)\nprint(make("Z").summary())\nprint(make("Q").owner)\naccounts = [Account(owner: "A"), Account(owner: "B", balance: 2)]\nfor a in accounts {\n    a.deposit(1)\n}\nprint(accounts[0].balance, accounts[1].balance)\n', 'tests/run-pass/classes.out': 'Asha: 125 true 125\nRavi: 10\n15\n25.0 3.0\n1 vip\nMaya: 7\nZ: 1\nQ\n1 3\n', 'tests/run-pass/collections_memory.kode': 'fn build(n: int) -> [string] {\n    out: [string] = []\n    for i in 0..n {\n        out.add("item" + string(i))\n    }\n    return out\n}\nfn count(words: [string]) -> int => len(words)\ntotal = 0\nfor r in 0..500 {\n    ws = build(10)\n    total += count(ws) + len(ws[3])\n}\nprint(total)\nm: {string: [int]} = {}\nm["a"] = [1, 2]\nm["b"] = [3]\nm["a"].add(9)\nprint(len(m["a"]), m["a"][2], len(m["b"]))\n', 'tests/run-pass/collections_memory.out': '7500\n3 9 1\n', 'tests/run-pass/enums_match.kode': 'enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }\n\nfn name(c: Color) -> string {\n    match c {\n        Color.Red => { return "red" }\n        Color.Green => { return "green" }\n        Color.Blue => { return "blue" }\n    }\n}\n\nc = Color.Green\nprint(name(c), name(Color.Blue), c == Color.Green, c != Color.Red)\nmatch c {\n    Color.Red => print("is red")\n    _ => print("not red")\n}\ncode = 404\nmatch code {\n    200 => print("ok")\n    404 => print("missing")\n    _ => print("other")\n}\nword = "go"\nmatch word {\n    "stop" => print("halt")\n    "go" => {\n        print("moving")\n    }\n    _ => print("?")\n}\nflag = true\nmatch flag {\n    true => print("yes")\n    false => print("no")\n}\n', 'tests/run-pass/enums_match.out': 'green blue true true\nnot red\nmissing\nmoving\nyes\n', 'tests/run-pass/errors.kode': 'fn parse_age(s: string) -> int {\n    if s == "bad" {\n        throw "not a number: ${s}"\n    }\n    if s == "neg" {\n        throw Error(message: "negative", code: 7)\n    }\n    return 42\n}\n\nfn wrapper(s: string) -> int {\n    x = parse_age(s)\n    return x + 1\n}\n\nprint(parse_age("ok"))\nv = parse_age("bad") catch e {\n    print("caught:", e.message)\n    -1\n}\nprint(v)\nw = wrapper("neg") catch e {\n    print("wrapped:", e.message, e.code)\n    0\n}\nprint(w)\ntry {\n    print("before")\n    parse_age("bad")\n    print("never")\n} catch err {\n    print("try-catch:", err.message)\n}\nfor i in 0..3 {\n    n = wrapper("ok") catch e {\n        continue\n    }\n    print(n)\n}\nfn safe(s: string) -> string {\n    r = parse_age(s) catch e {\n        return "failed: ${e.message}"\n    }\n    return "ok ${r}"\n}\nprint(safe("bad"), safe("fine"))\nfn nested() -> int {\n    try {\n        wrapper("bad")\n    } catch e {\n        throw "rethrown: ${e.message}"\n    }\n    return 1\n}\nmsg = nested() catch e {\n    print(e.message)\n    5\n}\nprint(msg)\n', 'tests/run-pass/errors.out': '42\ncaught: not a number: bad\n-1\nwrapped: negative 7\n0\nbefore\ntry-catch: not a number: bad\n43\n43\n43\nfailed: not a number: bad ok 42\nrethrown: not a number: bad\n5\n', 'tests/run-pass/lib/mathx.kode': 'const TAX = 5\n\npub fn double(x: int) -> int => x * 2\n\npub class Counter {\n    n: int = 0\n\n    fn inc() {\n        n += 1\n    }\n}\n\nenum Level { Low, High }\n\nfn secret() -> int => 7\n', 'tests/run-pass/lib/strings.kode': 'import "./mathx"\n\npub fn shout(s: string) -> string => s + "!" + string(double(1))\n', 'tests/run-pass/lists.kode': 'xs = [3, 1, 2]\nxs.add(10)\nprint(xs.len(), len(xs), xs[0], xs[3])\nxs[1] = 99\ntotal = 0\nfor x in xs {\n    total += x\n}\nprint(total)\nfor i, x in xs {\n    print(i, x)\n}\nnames: [string] = []\nnames.add("Asha")\nnames.add("Ravi" + "!")\nfor n in names {\n    print(n)\n}\nfs = [1.5, 2.5]\nprint(fs[0] + fs[1])\nbs = [true, false]\nprint(bs[0], bs[1], not bs[1])\nnested = [[1, 2], [3]]\nprint(nested[0][1], nested[1][0], len(nested))\nxs2: [int] = []\nprint(len(xs2))\n', 'tests/run-pass/lists.out': '4 4 3 10\n114\n0 3\n1 99\n2 2\n3 10\nAsha\nRavi!\n4.0\ntrue false true\n2 3 2\n0\n', 'tests/run-pass/maps.kode': 'ages = {"asha": 30, "ravi": 25}\nages["maya"] = 28\nprint(ages["asha"], ages["maya"], len(ages), ages.len())\nprint("ravi" in ages, "zed" in ages, ages.has("maya"))\nages["asha"] += 1\nprint(ages["asha"])\nfor k, v in ages {\n    print(k, v)\n}\nfor k in ages {\n    print(k)\n}\nks = ages.keys()\nprint(len(ks), ks[0])\nwords: {string: string} = {}\nwords["a"] = "alpha"\nwords["b"] = "beta" + "!"\nprint(words["a"], words["b"])\nwords["a"] = "ALPHA"\nprint(words["a"])\nscores = {"x": 1.5}\nprint(scores["x"] * 2.0)\n', 'tests/run-pass/maps.out': '30 28 3 3\ntrue false true\n31\nasha 31\nravi 25\nmaya 28\nasha\nravi\nmaya\n3 asha\nalpha beta!\nALPHA\n3.0\n', 'tests/run-pass/modules.kode': 'from "./lib/mathx" import double, Counter\nimport "./lib/strings"\n\nc = Counter()\nc.inc()\nc.inc()\nprint(double(21), c.n, TAX)\nprint(shout("hi"))\nlvl = Level.High\nprint(lvl == Level.High, secret())\n', 'tests/run-pass/modules.out': '42 2 5\nhi!2\ntrue 7\n', 'tests/run-pass/optionals.kode': 'class User {\n    name: string\n    nickname: string? = none\n    friend: User? = none\n}\n\nfn find(users: [User], name: string) -> User? {\n    for u in users {\n        if u.name == name {\n            return u\n        }\n    }\n    return none\n}\n\nusers = [User(name: "Asha"), User(name: "Ravi", nickname: "R")]\na = find(users, "Asha")\nz = find(users, "Zed")\nprint(a == none, z == none, a != none)\nprint(a!.name, (z ?? User(name: "default")).name)\nprint(users[1].nickname ?? "no nick", users[0].nickname ?? "no nick")\nprint(a?.nickname ?? "none")\nr: User? = users[1]\nprint(r?.name ?? "?")\ns: string? = none\nprint(s ?? "empty")\ns = "set"\nprint(s ?? "empty")\nusers[0].friend = users[1]\nprint(users[0].friend?.name ?? "alone")\nprint(users[1].friend?.name ?? "alone")\n', 'tests/run-pass/optionals.out': 'false true true\nAsha default\nR no nick\nnone\nRavi\nempty\nset\nRavi\nalone\n', 'AGENTS.md': '# Instructions for AI agents working on Kode\n\nKode is a compiled language written in Rust. Read `README.md` first.\n\n## Commands\n\n```\ncargo build --workspace     # build compiler and runtime (needed before running tests)\ncargo test --workspace      # run the conformance suite\n./target/debug/kode check file.kode --format=json   # structured errors\n./target/debug/kode mir file.kode                    # see what the compiler produced\n```\n\n## Where things live\n\n| To change... | Edit |\n|---|---|\n| tokens, keywords, string escapes | `crates/kode-syntax/src/token.rs`, `lexer.rs` |\n| syntax tree | `crates/kode-syntax/src/ast/` |\n| grammar | `crates/kode-syntax/src/parser/` (expr, stmt, item) |\n| type rules, error messages | `crates/kode-sema/src/check/` |\n| built-in functions | `kode-sema/src/builtins.rs`, `kode-mir/src/lower/expr.rs`, `kode-codegen/src/runtime.rs`, `kode-rt/src/` |\n| memory management (retain/release) | `crates/kode-mir/src/lower/mod.rs` (rules), `expr.rs`, `data.rs`, `stmt.rs` |\n| lists, maps, classes, optionals, errors in MIR | `crates/kode-mir/src/lower/data.rs` |\n| `kode test` runner | `crates/kode-mir/src/lower/tests.rs`, `crates/kode-cli/src/main.rs` |\n| imports (loading several files) | `crates/kode-cli/src/driver.rs`, `source.rs` |\n| `kode fmt`, `kode explain` | `crates/kode-cli/src/fmt.rs`, `explain.rs` (reads `docs/errors/index.md`) |\n| machine code | `crates/kode-codegen/src/function.rs` |\n| runtime functions | `crates/kode-rt/src/` (object, string, list, map, error, io); register new ones in `kode-mir/src/ir.rs` (`RtFn`) and `kode-codegen/src/runtime.rs` |\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Tests first.** A new feature starts as a `.kode` file with a `.out` file in `tests/run-pass/`,\n   or a `.kode` file with an `.err` file (the error code) in `tests/compile-fail/`.\n2. **Every error has a stable code** (`E0xxx`, warnings `W0xxx`). Add new codes to `docs/errors/index.md`.\n3. **Do not change language behavior without an ADR** in `docs/adr/`.\n4. **Only MIR reaches the backend.** Do not read the AST in `kode-codegen`.\n5. Strings are reference counted. If you add a feature that creates strings, follow the\n   ownership rules at the top of `crates/kode-mir/src/lower/mod.rs`, and make sure\n   `cargo test` (which sets `KODE_LEAK_CHECK=1`) still reports no leaks.\n6. Keep files under about 500 lines; split by idea.\n', 'CHANGELOG.md': '# Changelog\n\n## 0.2.0 (M2 to M5)\n- Lists `[T]` and maps `{string: T}`: literals, indexing, `.add`, `.len`, `in`, `.keys`, `for x in list`, `for k, v in map`.\n- Classes with fields, defaults, methods and named constructor arguments; enums; `match`.\n- Optionals `T?` for heap types with `none`, `?.`, `??`, `!`.\n- Errors: `throw`, `try / catch`, `expr catch e { ... }`, built-in `Error` class, errors that are freed on every path.\n- Named arguments for every call.\n- Imports across files (`import`, `from ... import`) with cycle and missing-file errors.\n- `test` blocks and `kode test`; `kode fmt`; `kode explain`; `kode mir` shows field loads and stores.\n- Runtime: one object header and one `release` for strings, lists, maps and objects; leak counter covers all of them.\n- MIR: single exit block per function, error edges that release live temporaries.\n- 19 run-pass programs (leak checked), 70 compile-fail programs, tests for `kode test`, `fmt`, `explain`.\n\n## 0.1.0 (first working core)\n- Rust rewrite: lexer, parser, type checker, MIR, Cranelift backend, runtime.\n- Types: int, float, bool, string. Block-scoped variables, constants.\n- Control flow: if / else if / else, while, for over ranges, break, continue.\n- Functions: parameters, return values, recursion, expression bodies.\n- Strings: reference counted, `+`, `${...}` interpolation, `len`, conversions.\n- Diagnostics with stable codes, "did you mean" hints and JSON output.\n- Conformance suite: 11 run-pass programs (leak checked) and 44 compile-fail programs.\n', 'README.md': '# Kode\n\n**A compiled language that is easy to write and cheap to run.**\nPython-readable, JavaScript-familiar, compiled to small native binaries,\nbuilt for cloud backends and for AI agents to write.\n\n```\nfn fib(n: int) -> int {\n    if n < 2 {\n        return n\n    }\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n}\n\nname = "Asha"\nprint("${name}: fib(30) =", fib(30))\n```\n\nKode is written in Rust. The compiler turns source into a native program:\n\n```\nsource -> lexer -> parser -> AST -> type check -> MIR -> Cranelift -> object file -> linker -> binary\n```\n\n## Status: v0.2 (working)\n\nWorks today: `int`, `float`, `bool`, `string`; variables and constants; operators; `if`, `while`,\n`for` over ranges, lists and maps, `break`, `continue`, `match`; functions with recursion and named\narguments; **lists** `[T]` and **maps** `{string: T}`; **classes** with methods and defaults;\n**enums**; **optionals** `T?` with `?.`, `??`, `!`; **errors** with `throw`, `try / catch` and\n`expr catch e { ... }`; **imports** across files; `test` blocks; reference-counted memory with a leak\nchecker; errors with codes, hints and JSON output.\n\nTools: `kode run | build | check | test | fmt | explain | mir`.\n\nNext: concurrency (`spawn` / `await`), an HTTP server and JSON, then budgets and AI features.\nSee [docs/ROADMAP.md](docs/ROADMAP.md) and the one-page [cheat sheet](docs/agents/cheatsheet.md).\n\nMeasured on one dev machine (recursive Fibonacci of 35): Kode about 0.06 s, Python about 0.9 to 1.8 s,\nrelease binary about 330 KB. Treat this as a first data point, not a claim. See `benchmarks/`.\n\n## Build and run\n\nNeeds Rust 1.91 or newer and a C compiler (`cc`). On a Mac: `xcode-select --install`.\n\n```\ncargo build --workspace\ncargo test --workspace\n./target/debug/kode run examples/hello.kode\n```\n\nTo type just `kode` in this terminal:\n\n```\nexport PATH="$PWD/target/debug:$PATH"\nkode run examples/tour.kode\n```\n\n## Commands\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `kode run file.kode` | compile and run |\n| `kode build file.kode [-o out] [--release]` | make a native binary |\n| `kode check file.kode` | errors only, no code generation |\n| `kode test [file or folder]` | run `test` blocks |\n| `kode fmt <files or folders> [--check]` | format source files |\n| `kode explain E0201` | explain an error code |\n| `kode tokens file.kode` | print tokens (debugging) |\n| `kode ast file.kode` | print the syntax tree (debugging) |\n| `kode mir file.kode` | print the MIR, including retain/release (debugging) |\n| `--format=json` | diagnostics as JSON, for editors and AI agents |\n| `--strict` | warnings count as errors |\n\n## Repository map\n\n```\ncrates/\n  kode-diagnostics/   spans and error messages (human + JSON)\n  kode-syntax/        tokens, lexer, AST, parser\n  kode-sema/          names and types\n  kode-mir/           mid-level IR, lowering, dump, verifier\n  kode-codegen/       MIR -> Cranelift -> object file -> linker\n  kode-rt/            runtime library linked into every program\n  kode-cli/           the `kode` command\ntests/run-pass/       programs that must run (.kode + .out), checked for leaks\ntests/compile-fail/   programs that must fail (.kode + .err with the error code)\nexamples/             small programs\nbenchmarks/           proof of the cost claims\ndocs/                 spec, design notes, decisions (ADRs), roadmap, error codes\n```\n\n## License\n\nMIT\n', 'docs/ROADMAP.md': '# Roadmap\n\nGoal: a language that serves more requests per cloud server than Java, Node and Python,\nis simple to write, and is easy for AI agents to use.\n\nTime budget assumption: under 10 hours a week, so each milestone is small and ends with a\nworking, tested compiler.\n\n| Milestone | Content | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **M1 Core** | int, float, bool, string; variables, const; operators; if, while, for ranges; functions and recursion; reference-counted strings; MIR; Cranelift; diagnostics with codes and JSON; conformance suite | **Done** |\n| **M2 Collections** | `[T]` lists and `{string: T}` maps, indexing, `for x in list`, `.add`, `.len()`, `in`, `.keys()` (sorting, `map` and `filter` still to come) | **Done** |\n| **M3 Classes** | `class` with fields and methods, named constructors, reference counting for objects, enums, `match` (`weak` fields still to come) | **Done** |\n| **M4 Errors and optionals** | `throw` / `catch` / `try`, `T?` for heap types, `none`, `?.`, `??`, `!` (`finally`, `int?` and narrowing still to come) | **Done** |\n| **M5 Tooling** | `import` and modules, `test` blocks and `kode test`, `kode fmt`, `kode explain`, agent cheat sheet (project file and qualified module names still to come) | **Done** |\n| **M6 Concurrency** | `spawn` / `await`, lightweight tasks, channels, atomic reference counts, `shared` classes | Planned |\n| **M7 Services** | `serve` / routes, HTTP and JSON in the standard library, database client, the REST benchmark against Go, Node, Python and Java | Planned |\n| **M8 Budgets and AI** | `memory` budgets, declared effects (`uses`), typed `ask` / `tool` calls | Planned |\n| **M9 Release** | LLVM backend for `--release` (optional), Windows, language server, docs site, open source | Planned |\n\n## Checkpoints (go / no-go)\n\n- After M2: the same small program in Kode and Python, compared on speed and lines of code. (`examples/bank.kode` is the first candidate.)\n- After M7: the REST benchmark on a 1 vCPU / 1 GB VM. Kode must clearly beat Python and Node on\n  requests per second and RAM, and be close to Go. If not, change the plan before M8.\n\n## Principles\n\n1. The conformance suite is the source of truth for the language.\n2. One backend input: MIR.\n3. Every error has a code and a hint.\n4. Measure before claiming.\n', 'docs/design/architecture.md': '# Architecture\n\n```\n          kode-diagnostics  (Span, Diagnostic: shared by all stages)\n                 |\nsource -> kode-syntax -> kode-sema -> kode-mir -> kode-codegen -> object file -> cc -> binary\n          lex, parse     types        CFG          Cranelift                      + kode-rt\n                                                                     \nkode-cli drives the pipeline (and loads imported files into one program, formats code, runs tests,\nexplains errors).   kode-rt is the runtime library every binary links against.\n```\n\n| Crate | Input | Output |\n|---|---|---|\n| `kode-syntax` | text | `Program` (AST) |\n| `kode-sema` | AST | `Checked`: type of every expression, function signatures, constants, warnings |\n| `kode-mir` | AST + `Checked` | `MirProgram` |\n| `kode-codegen` | `MirProgram` | native object file, then an executable |\n| `kode-rt` | | static library with print, strings, runtime errors |\n\nRules:\n\n1. Each crate depends only on the ones before it. Replacing the backend never touches the parser.\n2. `kode-codegen` reads only MIR.\n3. Errors are data (`Diagnostic` with a code), never panics.\n4. Generated names: user function `f` becomes `kode_fn_f`, so it can never clash with a C library symbol.\n\n## Adding a feature\n\n1. Write the test program (`tests/run-pass` or `tests/compile-fail`).\n2. Token and lexer, AST node, parser rule.\n3. Type rules in `kode-sema`, with error codes.\n4. Lowering in `kode-mir` (and the ownership rules if it creates strings or objects).\n5. Backend support only if MIR gained a new instruction.\n', 'docs/design/memory-model.md': '# Memory model\n\nEvery heap value (string, list, map, class instance) is **reference counted**. The compiler\ninserts `retain` and `release` in MIR; users never write them.\n\nAll heap values share one header: `[refcount: i64][kind: i64]`. A refcount of -1 marks a static\nvalue (string literals) that is never freed. The `kind` says what it is, so one `release`\nfunction can free anything, including the items inside a list or the fields of an object.\n\n| Kind | Layout after the header |\n|---|---|\n| string | `[length][bytes...]` |\n| list | `[length][capacity][pointer to 8-byte items]`; a flag says whether the items are heap values |\n| map | `[pointer to the runtime map]` (string keys, insertion order); same flag for values |\n| class instance | `[refmask][fields...]`, 8 bytes per field; `refmask` says which fields are heap values |\n\n## Rules (`crates/kode-mir/src/lower/mod.rs`)\n\n1. A value is **owned** (we hold a reference) or **borrowed** (a variable, a literal, a field).\n2. Storing in a variable, a field, a list or a map, or returning, takes ownership\n   (`retain` if it was only borrowed).\n3. Assigning to a variable first releases the old value. Variables start as `null`; releasing\n   null is a no-op.\n4. Every function has **one exit block** that releases all its heap variables. `return`\n   jumps there, so no path can forget to release.\n5. Parameters are borrowed. Callers keep ownership of what they pass.\n6. Temporaries are released right after use.\n7. **Errors:** if an error is raised while a temporary is alive, the error path releases it too\n   (the lowering tracks the live temporaries as `pending`).\n8. A collection or object that is being looped over is held in a hidden variable so it cannot\n   be freed during the loop.\n\n## Checking it\n\nThe runtime counts live objects. `KODE_LEAK_CHECK=1` makes a program exit with code 3 if any\nobject is left at the end. `cargo test` sets it for every run-pass program.\n\n## Known limits\n\n- A variable lives until its function exits or it is re-assigned, not until its block ends.\n- Counts are not atomic yet; concurrency (M6) will make them atomic.\n- Two objects that point at each other leak. `weak` references are planned.\n', 'docs/design/mir.md': '# MIR\n\nMIR is a control-flow graph. A function is a list of **locals** and **basic blocks**.\nA block is a list of simple instructions and one terminator.\n\n```\nfn fib(_0: int) -> int {\n    let _1: bool\n  bb0:\n    _1 = lt _0, 2\n    branch _1, bb1, bb2\n  bb1:\n    return _0\n  bb2:\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nPrint it with `kode mir file.kode`.\n\n| Instruction | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `dst = use x`, unary, binary, cast | arithmetic and moves on locals and constants |\n| `dst = call f(args)` | call a user function or a runtime function (`rt::...`) |\n| `dst = load base+offset` / `store base+offset, x` | read and write a field of a class instance |\n| `retain x` / `release x` | change the owner count of a heap value |\n\n| Terminator | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `goto bb` | jump |\n| `branch c, bbA, bbB` | conditional jump |\n| `return [x]` | leave the function |\n| `unreachable` | cannot happen (after a diverging call) |\n\n`and`, `or`, `??`, `if`, `while`, `for`, `match`, `try` / `catch` and all error handling are lowered to blocks and jumps here.\nA value of type `ref` is any reference-counted heap value.\n`kode_mir::verify` checks the graph after lowering; a failure is a compiler bug.\n\nWhy a MIR: memory management, control flow and (later) optimizations are far easier on\nsimple blocks than on a tree, and any backend (Cranelift, LLVM, WebAssembly) can read it.\n', 'docs/errors/index.md': '# Error codes\n\nEvery message has a stable code. `kode check --format=json` prints them as JSON, and\n`kode explain E0201` shows a longer explanation with an example. Each code has a test in\n`tests/compile-fail/`.\n\n## Lexer (E000x)\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| E0001 | string is never closed |\n| E0002 | unknown escape sequence |\n| E0003 | `${` is never closed |\n| E0004 | block comment is never closed |\n| E0005 | integer literal is too large |\n| E0006 | not a valid number |\n| E0007 | unexpected character (including `;`) |\n\n## Parser (E01xx)\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| E0101 | expected the end of the line |\n| E0102 | expected a value |\n| E0103 | expected a specific token such as `)` or `{` |\n| E0105 | function defined inside a block |\n| E0106 | block is never closed |\n| E0107 | expected `=` after a declared type |\n| E0108 | a range loop has exactly one variable |\n| E0110 | expected a name |\n| E0111 | missing or invalid type |\n| E0112 | this cannot be assigned to |\n| E0114 | expected a field or a method in a class |\n| E0115 | map keys must be strings |\n| E0116 | expected a quoted file path or name |\n\n## Type checker (E02xx)\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| E0201 | unknown variable |\n| E0202 | wrong type in assignment or declaration |\n| E0203 | operator or function cannot be used with these types |\n| E0204 | division by zero (constant) |\n| E0205 | a call that returns nothing was used as a value |\n| E0206 | unknown function |\n| E0207 | wrong number of arguments |\n| E0208 | wrong argument type |\n| E0209 | condition is not a bool |\n| E0210 | int and float mixed without conversion |\n| E0211 | this value cannot be indexed |\n| E0212 | wrong index type (lists need an int, maps need a string) |\n| E0213 | list or map items have different types |\n| E0214 | cannot loop over this value |\n| E0215 | bad named argument |\n| E0216 | missing argument |\n| E0217 | unknown field or method |\n| E0218 | this value has no fields |\n| E0219 | optional type or `?.` not supported for this type |\n| E0220 | value may be none and cannot be used directly |\n| E0221 | `?.` or `??` used on something that is not an optional |\n| E0222 | an empty list or map needs a declared type |\n| E0223 | bad `match` value or pattern type |\n| E0224 | `match` does not cover every case |\n| E0225 | unknown enum variant |\n| E0226 | `throw` needs a string or an `Error` |\n| E0227 | `catch` block does not end with a value |\n| E0228 | parameter has the same name as a field |\n\n## Declarations and statements (E03xx)\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| E0301 | function, class, enum or method defined twice |\n| E0302 | name is a built-in function |\n| E0303 | unknown type name |\n| E0304 | `=>` body does not match the declared type |\n| E0305 | not every path returns a value |\n| E0306 | parameter or field name used twice |\n| E0307 | constant is not a plain value |\n| E0308 | constant defined twice |\n| E0310 | `return` value has the wrong type |\n| E0311 | `return` outside a function |\n| E0312 | assignment to a constant |\n| E0313 | variable already declared |\n| E0314 | `break` or `continue` outside a loop |\n\n## Modules (E04xx)\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| E0401 | imported file not found |\n| E0402 | import cycle |\n| E0403 | a module has top-level code |\n| E0405 | the module does not define this name |\n\n## Warnings\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| W0001 | variable assigned but never used (`--strict` turns it into an error) |\n', 'docs/spec/01-lexical.md': '# 1. Lexical structure\n\n- Source files end in `.kode` and are UTF-8.\n- A statement ends at the end of the line. There are no semicolons (`;` is an error, E0007).\n- A line continues when it ends with a binary operator or a comma, or when it is inside `( )`.\n- Comments: `// to end of line` and `/* block */`.\n- Identifiers: letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit.\n- Keywords: `fn const if else while for in break continue return true false and or not class enum match import from pub throw try catch none test`.\n- Integers: `42`, `1_000`. Floats: `3.14`, `2.5e-3`, `1e6`. A range `1..5` is not a float.\n- Strings: `"text"` on one line. Escapes: `\\n \\t \\r \\0 \\" \\\\ \\$`.\n  Interpolation: `"${expression}"`; any expression may go inside, including nested strings.\n', 'docs/spec/04-statements.md': '# 4. Statements\n\n```\nif score >= 90 {\n    grade = "A"\n} else if score >= 75 {\n    grade = "B"\n} else {\n    grade = "C"\n}\n\nwhile n < 10 {\n    n += 1\n}\n\nfor i in 0..5 { print(i) }        # 0 1 2 3 4\nfor i in 0..=5 { print(i) }       # 0 to 5\n```\n\n- `else` must be on the same line as the closing `}`.\n- A range end is evaluated once. An empty range (`5..2`) runs zero times.\n- `break` leaves the nearest loop. `continue` jumps to the next round (E0314 outside loops).\n- Braces are required. Parentheses around conditions are not.\n- Top-level statements run in order, like a script.\n- More statements: `for x in list`, `match`, `throw`, `try / catch`; see chapters 8 to 10.\n', 'docs/spec/05-functions.md': '# 5. Functions\n\n```\nfn add(a: int, b: int) -> int {\n    return a + b\n}\n\nfn square(x: int) -> int => x * x      # one expression\n\nfn log(msg: string) {                  # returns nothing: no `->`\n    print("log:", msg)\n}\n```\n\n- Functions are defined at the top level only and may be used before their definition.\n- Parameters must have types. **The return type must be written when the function returns a value.**\n  This is a deliberate rule: the signature is the contract, which also helps AI agents (ADR 0007).\n- Every path of a value-returning function must `return` (E0305).\n- Recursion and mutual recursion work.\n- Arguments can be named: `greet(name: "Asha")`. Positional arguments come first.\n- A function that can fail (`throw`, or a call to one that can) just propagates errors to its caller.\n- Built-in functions: `print(...)`, `len(string | list | map)`, `int(x)`, `float(x)`, `string(x)`, `assert(cond)`.\n  They cannot be redefined (E0302).\n- `print` takes any number of values, separates them with a space and ends with a new line.\n', 'docs/spec/06-strings-and-memory.md': '# 6. Strings and memory\n\nStrings are immutable and reference counted. There is no garbage collector and no manual freeing.\n\n```\ns = "Hello" + ", " + name           # new string\nt = "${s} (${len(s)} chars)"         # interpolation\n```\n\n- `len(s)` counts characters, not bytes.\n- A string is freed as soon as the last variable that holds it is re-assigned or its function ends.\n- Function parameters are borrowed, so passing a string never copies it.\n- Run any program with `KODE_LEAK_CHECK=1` to make the runtime fail when a string was not freed.\n  The test suite does this for every run-pass program.\n\nLists, maps and class instances follow the same rules. Design details: `docs/design/memory-model.md`.\n', 'docs/spec/07-planned.md': '# 7. Planned (not implemented yet)\n\n```\ntask = spawn fetch(1)                 # M6: concurrency\nprice = await task\n\nserve(8080) {                         # M7: services\n    get("/todos") => todos\n}\n```\n\n- M6: `spawn` / `await`, channels, atomic reference counts, `shared` classes.\n- M7: HTTP server, JSON, database client, string functions (`split`, `upper`, ...), file I/O.\n- M8: resource budgets (`memory 64mb`), declared effects (`uses db`), typed `ask` / `tool` calls for AI.\n- Later: `weak` references, interfaces, `finally`, `int?`, optional narrowing, integer map keys,\n  list sorting and `map`/`filter`, qualified module names, a package manager, a language server.\n'}


def main():
    if not os.path.exists("Cargo.toml"):
        sys.exit("Run this from the root of the Kode repository (the folder with Cargo.toml).")
    for path in DELETE:
        if os.path.exists(path):
            os.remove(path)
            print("removed", path)
    for path, text in FILES.items():
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path) or ".", exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n") as f:
            f.write(text)
    print("wrote", len(FILES), "files")


main()